  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-a:text-blue-700 prose-a:underline">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1>Building This Library</h1>

    <p>
      America's School Trust Library now has a legal home of its own, and a
      board to answer for it. Oregon accepted its Articles of Incorporation on
      July 21, 2026, creating an Oregon nonprofit public benefit corporation
      (Registry No. 259005891). On August 3, 2026 the founding board was seated
      and adopted the Library's governing instruments by unanimous written
      consent. The federal tax-exemption process is under way; no application
      has been filed and no determination has been issued.
    </p>

    <h2>Why an independent institution</h2>

    <p>
      The Library grew out of Oregon Advocates for School Trust Lands, but a
      national library must be answerable to its readers and beneficiaries,
      not to a single lawsuit or founder. Independence protects the record,
      invites collaboration across institutional lines, and gives the Library
      a structure designed to outlive any one person, case, or political
      season.
    </p>

    <h2>What has been completed</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Oregon Department of Justice Charitable Activities Registration No. 72571 was completed July 10, 2026.</li>
      <li>Oregon accepted the Articles of Incorporation on July 21, 2026 (Registry No. 259005891).</li>
      <li>The corporation is a public benefit corporation with no members.</li>
      <li>The Articles contain the provisions required for a charitable, educational organization.</li>
      <li>The corporation obtained its federal Employer Identification Number on July 30, 2026 and opened its institutional records.</li>
      <li>
        The founding board was seated on August 3, 2026 and adopted the
        founding-board package by unanimous written consent:
        <a href="/bylaws/">Bylaws, Version 7</a>; the
        <a href="/rights/">Constitution and Readers' Bill of Rights,
        Version 1</a>; and a Conflict of Interest Policy, Version 2. All three
        are published in full, as adopted, on the
        <a href="/governance/">governance page</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        On August 9, 2026 the board added Barry Shane as a director and
        accepted the withdrawal of Barbara K. Sullivan, who had served as
        founding treasurer. The current roster is on the
        <a href="/about/">About</a> page; the full dated record of every filing
        and board action is the <a href="/organizing/">organizational
        record</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What comes next</h2>

    <ol>
      <li>The board approves and files the appropriate federal tax-exemption application.</li>
      <li>The board grows toward the broader, independent governing body the Library needs for the long term. Two director seats are open now, and a Law Librarian seat authorized by the bylaws is open as well.</li>
      <li>The board takes up this publication itself.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      That last step is the one still outstanding, and it is why this site
      remains a preview publication. Seating a board and adopting its
      instruments settles what the corporation is; it does not settle what
      this site says. The board has not yet endorsed this publication, so
      every page here carries a preview notice, and the primary sources
      behind each page remain the thing to cite.
    </p>

    <h2>An open invitation</h2>

    <p>
      Readers, librarians, archivists, educators, trust professionals, and
      peer institutions are still invited to help shape the durable
      institution — the more so now that there is a board to receive the help.
      Two director seats and the Law Librarian seat are open; the ways to
      write to the Library about them, and the lighter ways to contribute, are
      on the <a href="/contribute/">Contribute</a> page. Use the
      <strong>Submit Feedback</strong> control on this page, or begin with the
      contact paths on the <a href="/about/">About</a> page. This page is not a
      fundraising solicitation, and the Library does not yet represent that
      contributions are federally tax deductible.
    </p>

    <p class="signature">— <em>Dave Sullivan, President, OASTL</em></p>

    <p class="page-stamp">
      <em>Page last updated: 2026-08-09.</em>
    </p>
  </article>

